    "question": {
      ":question/id": "688c3a50-32ad-5270-8414-4ef95a767bb1",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186129147/plots/936a0910f46e43e1b9d600105c13dbfc223ebd3b53d1506042c7baa7df7b3b20.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-(x-3)(x-2)^{2}(x-4)^{3}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=2$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=3$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=3$ is a simple root, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=4$ is $3,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=4$ is a triple-root, the graph must be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$ Since only graph $\\text{I}$ displays this end behavior, the correct answer is $\\text{I}.$",
